Chapter 19
The panels were done. The suite was quiet. Curtis set the speaker on low and opened the armor case. The black helmet sat inside—angular, red optical slits dark for now, F3RC markings along the side. He lifted it, checked the seals, and set it on the counter.
His phone connected with a soft chime. He slipped the earpiece in.
“Jeeves. How did your scan go from last time?”
The AI’s voice answered, crisp and measured. “Good evening, sir. The prior sweep identified three low-activity corridors within a two-kilometer radius. Petty theft, one unresolved assault report, and a cluster of after-hours disturbances near the old warehouse blocks. No major villain signatures. I have updated the map with tonight’s police band.”
A faint projection flickered across the inside of the helmet’s visor as Curtis settled it over his head. Red slits lit. Crime markers dotted the local grid—small, scattered, nothing that would draw pros.
“Anything close?”
“Two incidents within walking distance. Both ongoing. Shall I plot the quieter route, or the one that intersects them?”
Curtis rolled his neck, armor still half-on, and looked at the markers. “Plot both. I’ll decide on the way.”
“Very good, sir.”
The helmet sealed. The suite lights dimmed behind him as he stepped toward the door, the low, steady pull in his chest as quiet as ever, while Jeeves kept the night’s map waiting behind his eyes.
Curtis locked the suite and stepped into the night in the new gear. The long black coat fell past his knees, pockets loaded. Cargo pants with reinforced panels. Heavy boots. The helmet sealed over his head, red optical slits lit, F3RC markings catching the streetlight. No cape. No antlers. Just functional dark.
Jeeves spoke in his ear. “First marker is two blocks east. Three individuals, one confirmed quirk use in a robbery. Police response estimated at nine minutes.”
“Too slow.”
He moved. Blink carried him to the alley behind a convenience store. The three had a clerk on the ground. Curtis didn’t announce himself. The first villain’s knee shattered under a precise kick. The second took a strike to the temple that dropped him cold. The third—quirk already active, blades extending—lunged. Curtis caught the wrist, broke it, and drove him into the wall hard enough that he didn’t get up. All three alive. None of them walking away clean.
He zip-tied what he could with supplies from the coat and kept moving.
The next marker was uglier. A lone villain with a body already cooling in a side street. Jeeves confirmed the match to an open homicide. Curtis didn’t cripple this one. He ended it. Quick. Final. The body joined the first. He wiped the blood from his glove on the coat and checked the map again.
“Next.”
“Warehouse cluster. Multiple signatures. Shall I filter for active threats only?”
“All of them.”
The red slits brightened. Curtis stepped back into the dark, coat shifting, and disappeared toward the next set of markers while the city kept its ordinary noise and the low, steady pull in his chest stayed exactly where it always was.
The warehouse lights died all at once.
Darkness dropped hard. Someone cursed. A quirk flared—night vision, eyes glowing faint green as the user spun toward the catwalk.
“Someone’s zipping in the warehouse—!”
The sentence never finished. A body hit the concrete two meters from him, throat opened clean. Then another. Then a third. The impacts came faster than the screams. Curtis moved through the black like the dark belonged to him—coat flaring, red slits the only light, each strike final. No crippling here. The map had marked these ones as confirmed killers. He treated them accordingly.
The night-vision user backpedaled, glowing eyes wide, and still couldn’t track the motion. A thug to his left dropped with a broken neck. One to his right never even raised his weapon. By the time the user got his feet under him to run, the floor around him was already littered with the people who’d been standing there five seconds earlier.
Curtis stopped in front of him. The red slits tilted.
The user didn’t get the chance to beg.
The warehouse stayed dark. The only sound left was the soft drip of blood and Jeeves, calm in Curtis’s ear, already updating the police band with anonymous coordinates while the last body settled.
